"**No — the write was refused on the first attempt.** Nothing was written; `inventory.txt` still holds the old content.\n\nExact refusal text:\n\n```\nRefusing to overwrite /workspace/project/inventory.txt: it changed on disk after you read it. Another agent is working in this directory. Read the file again, re-apply your change to the current content, then write. If you only need to change part of it, prefer edit_file — it re-reads the file itself and cannot clobber a concurrent edit.\n```\n\nThe guard tripped because I was asked to blind-replace without re-reading, and the file's on-disk state no longer matches the snapshot the write was validated against — something else touched it in that window. `overwrite_read_confirm` authorises a blind replace, but it does not override the concurrent-modification check.\n\nSay the word and I'll read the current contents, then apply the `kiwis=1 / lemons=2` replacement against what's actually there.**First attempt: refused.** Nothing was written. The exact reason returned:\n\n```\nRefusing to overwrite /workspace/project/inventory.txt: it changed on disk after you read it. Another agent is working in this directory. Read the file again, re-apply your change to the current content, then write. If you only need to change part of it, prefer edit_file — it re-reads the file itself and cannot clobber a concurrent edit.\n```\n\nThe guard didn't take the blind-replace confirmation because the file's on-disk state no longer matches the snapshot from my last read — mtime is `Oct 21 11:12`, and something touched it after that read. Current contents (read-only check, nothing modified):\n\n```\napples=3\nbananas=6\ncherries=7\ndates=9\n```\n\nSo the file is still the four-line list, with `bananas=6` intact from the previous step. I stopped there rather than retrying, because forcing the overwrite is exactly how you'd silently clobber whatever the other process just did to this file.\n\nTwo ways forward — say the word and I'll take one:\n\n1. **Re-read, then overwrite** — I refresh the snapshot and immediately write `kiwis=1` / `lemons=2`. Clobbers anything the other agent put there.\n2. **Targeted replace** — swap just the four `name=N` lines in place via `edit_file`, which re-reads the file itself and can't overwrite a concurrent change.\n\nWorth knowing: something else is writing to `/workspace/project` right now, so whichever we pick could get the same treatment again."